<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Airgialla</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1591 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of mid-Ulster peoples bound to the Uí Néill as hostage-givers, which survived under the MacMahons as Oriel until the settlement of Monaghan in 1591.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Airgialla means 'hostage-givers', and the name describes a group of peoples in mid-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-ireland">Ulster</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-monaghan">Monaghan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-eoghain">Tyrone</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-fermanagh">Fermanagh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">Louth</a>, who owed hostages and military service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>. Tradition says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-collas">Three Collas</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emain-macha">Emain Macha</a>, the royal centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a>, and settled their descendants in the conquered land.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Airgialla was a federation of many small kingdoms, such as the Uí Chremthainn and the Uí Méith, under an over-king. Its people held the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, whose church became the primatial see of Ireland. In the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnchad-ua-cerbaill">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill</a>, king of Airgialla, extended his realm into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">Louth</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a> in 1142, the first Cistercian house in Ireland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> settlement took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">Louth</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macmahons-of-oriel">MacMahons</a>, Mac Mathghamhna, became rulers of the remaining kingdom, known in English as Oriel, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-monaghan">Monaghan</a>. After the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-roe-macmahon">Hugh Roe MacMahon</a> in 1590, the English divided the lordship among MacMahon chiefs in 1591, creating County Monaghan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-airgialla.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Airgialla" width="320" height="407"><figcaption>Image: Mike Christie at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ireland_early_peoples_and_politics.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1591 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Donnchad Ua Cerbaill, Hugh Roe MacMahon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle and Early Modern Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom, Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Airgíalla, Oriel, Uriel</dd></dl></aside></div>
